--TEST--
`Core\Response::text` is not a sink, so a tainted body is accepted where an unclassified `string` parameter would refuse it -- ADR 0088 §§ 2, 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0088 § 2: an unclassified `string` parameter refuses a tainted argument.
// § 4's table classifies `text`'s body contagious instead, and ADR 0074 is why
// it may -- `nosniff` is on with nothing configured, so a `text/plain` body is
// never re-parsed as HTML by a browser.
tainted string $t = "from the wire" as tainted string;
Core\Response::text($t);
echo "\n";

// The classification is a property of the parameter, not of the argument: the
// same member takes a plain `string` and nothing about the body changes.
Core\Response::text("from a literal");
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
from the wire
from a literal
